32	 * Traceability sensor for {@code docs/use_cases/}.
33	 *
34	 * <p>{@code docs/} is the source of truth, but until now nothing connected it
35	 * to the tests: a use case could claim {@code Status: Done} while an
36	 * alternative flow or a business rule had never been exercised, and a renamed
37	 * flow in a specification would silently orphan the {@code @UseCase} annotation
38	 * pointing at it. This test closes that loop in both directions.
39	 *
40	 * <p><strong>Referential integrity</strong>, every {@code @UseCase} annotation
41	 * must point at a use case, a scenario, and business rules that actually exist
42	 * in {@code docs/use_cases/}. This applies to every specification regardless of
43	 * status, because a dangling reference is always a defect.
44	 *
45	 * <p><strong>Coverage</strong>, a specification whose status is in
46	 * {@link #CLAIMS_COMPLETE} must additionally have a test behind its main
47	 * success scenario, behind every alternative flow, and behind every business
48	 * rule. Specifications with any other status are exempt: this project writes
49	 * the specification first, so an unimplemented use case is a normal
50	 * intermediate state, not a violation. The status line is what turns the
51	 * coverage requirement on, which is precisely why it must not be set by hand
52	 * without evidence.
53	 *
54	 * <p>Failures list every violation at once rather than stopping at the first,
55	 * so the report reads as a work list.
